\title{A Conditional-Impossibility Framework\\ for Classical Integer Factoring}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous conditional-impossibility schema for classical integer
factorization, organised into three logical levels that are kept strictly apart.
At Level~1 we prove unconditional theorems: the congruence-of-squares reduction
--- the structural core shared by every general-purpose classical method and by
the classical post-processing of period finding --- is unconditional and
computationally free, so all difficulty is concentrated in \emph{producing} a
congruence of squares rather than exploiting one; the subexponential complexity
functions $\Lf[\alpha,c](x)=\exp(c\,x^{\alpha}(\log x)^{1-\alpha})$ are
superpolynomial for $0<\alpha\le 1$ and subexponential for $0<\alpha<1$, hence
strictly intermediate between the polynomial and exponential rungs, while
$\exp(bx)$ with $b>0$ is superpolynomial and \emph{not} subexponential; a
multiplicative trade-off theorem shows, by two applications of AM--GM, that a
$k$-stage strategy under a multiplicative budget constraint $\prod_i y_i=x$
costs at least $k\exp(x^{1/k})$ with equality at the balanced point, so the
sieve exponents $1/2$ and $1/3$ are forced balance points rather than design
parameters, together with the honest boundary that unbounded arity collapses the
bound to $O(\log x)$; the arithmetic trajectory is provably blind for
$\min(p,q)\approx\sqrt N$ steps, so the celebrated $N^{1/4}$ figure for
collision methods is average-case only; and any family of $K$ Fourier sample
frequencies determining every period-$r$ signal on $\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$
satisfies $K\ge r$, with sharpness at $K=r$.
At Level~2 we prove the conditional-impossibility chain: if a classical
algorithm factors semiprimes in time polynomially bounded in $\log N$, then
every classified barrier exceeds its cost by an unbounded factor, it is limited
by none of them, and the resource it exploits lies outside
$\{\Rand,\Smooth,\Iter,\Analog\}$.
At Level~3 we isolate, as an explicit \emph{hypothesis} and not a theorem, the
Classified Resource Hypothesis; we prove that it implies the nonexistence of
polynomial-time classical factoring, that any polynomial-time algorithm
falsifies it, and that the resulting schema is non-vacuous.
The framework is a classification of the known, not a proof that the unknown is
empty. It establishes the precise sense in which a fast classical factoring
algorithm would require a genuinely novel resource, and identifies quantum
superposition as the only known resource evading all four barriers --- the
bound $K\ge r$ being exactly the obstruction that superposition circumvents.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} integer factorization; subexponential complexity;
congruence of squares; AM--GM trade-off; Fourier sampling lower bound;
conditional impossibility; asymptotic growth classes.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem and the epistemic situation}

Given a semiprime $N=pq$ with $p,q$ distinct large primes, recover $p$ and $q$.
The presumed hardness of this problem underwrites a substantial fraction of
deployed public-key cryptography. Yet the hardness is not a theorem. No
superpolynomial lower bound for factoring is known in any general model of
classical computation, and none is in sight; the belief in hardness rests on a
research record rather than a proof.

This paper takes the epistemic situation itself as its object. Rather than
attempt an unconditional lower bound --- which would settle questions far beyond
factoring --- we ask a more tractable and, we argue, more informative question:
\emph{if} a polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm exists, \emph{what
must it be made of}? The answer we obtain is: not any of the four resources that
all known classical methods use. This is a theorem, and it is a conditional. Its
value lies in converting the informal observation ``many clever attacks have
failed'' into a precise structural statement about \emph{where} they fail and
\emph{what kind of novelty} a success would require.

\subsection{Three logical levels, kept apart}

A recurring failure mode in the literature surrounding factoring barriers is the
silent slide from ``all known methods hit a wall'' to ``all methods hit a
wall.'' We structure the framework so that the slide is syntactically
impossible.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Level 1 --- Unconditional theorems.} Statements proved outright,
  with no hypothesis about the space of algorithms.
  \item \textbf{Level 2 --- Conditional impossibility.} A proved implication
  whose antecedent is the existence of a polynomial-time classical algorithm and
  whose consequent constrains the resource it must exploit.
  \item \textbf{Level 3 --- Scope, stated as a hypothesis.} The Classified
  Resource Hypothesis, asserting exhaustiveness of the classification. It is
  explicitly \emph{not} proved; we prove only its consequences and its
  falsifiability.
\end{itemize}

\section{The structural core: congruences of squares}

\begin{definition}[Nontrivial divisor]
For naturals $N,d$, say $d$ is a \emph{nontrivial divisor} of $N$ if $d\mid N$,
$1<d$ and $d<N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Splitting]
If $d$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N$, then $N=de$ for some $e$ with $d>1$ and
$e>1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $N=de$. If $e=0$ then $N=0$, contradicting $d<N$; if $e=1$ then $d=N$,
again contradicting $d<N$. Hence $e\ge 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Congruence of squares]\label{thm:cos}
Let $N>1$ and let $x,y\in\mathbb{Z}$ satisfy
\[
  N\mid (x-y)(x+y),\qquad N\nmid (x-y),\qquad N\nmid (x+y).
\]
Then $\gcd(x-y,N)$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set $d=\gcd(x-y,N)$, so $d\mid N$. If $d=1$ then $x-y$ is coprime to $N$, and
cancelling the coprime factor in $N\mid(x-y)(x+y)$ yields $N\mid (x+y)$, contrary
to hypothesis. If $d=N$ then $N\mid (x-y)$, also contrary to hypothesis. Since
$d\mid N$ and $N>1$ we have $d\ne 0$; hence $1<d$, and $d\le N$ with $d\ne N$
gives $d<N$.
\end{proof}

The hypotheses are not decorative. With $N=15$, $x=4$, $y=11$ we have
$15\mid (4-11)(4+11)=-105$, yet $\gcd(4-11,15)=1$ is not a nontrivial divisor:
here $x\equiv -y \pmod{15}$, and dropping $N\nmid (x+y)$ destroys the conclusion.

\begin{theorem}[Order-finding reduction]\label{thm:order}
Let $N>1$, $a\in\mathbb{Z}$, $s\in\mathbb{N}$ with
$N\mid a^{2s}-1$, $N\nmid a^{s}-1$, $N\nmid a^{s}+1$. Then $\gcd(a^{s}-1,N)$ is a
nontrivial divisor of $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(a^{s}-1)(a^{s}+1)=a^{2s}-1$, so this is Theorem~\ref{thm:cos} with $x=a^{s}$,
$y=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semiprime rigidity]\label{thm:semirigid}
Let $p,q$ be primes and $d$ a nontrivial divisor of $pq$. Then $d=p$ or $d=q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $p\mid d$, write $d=pk$; then $pk\mid pq$ gives $k\mid q$, so $k\in\{1,q\}$.
The case $k=q$ gives $d=pq$, contradicting $d<pq$; hence $d=p$. If $p\nmid d$
then $p$ and $d$ are coprime, so $d\mid pq$ forces $d\mid q$, and $d>1$ gives
$d=q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A congruence of squares modulo a semiprime \emph{is} a factorization of that
semiprime.
\end{corollary}

Theorems~\ref{thm:cos}--\ref{thm:semirigid} cost a single gcd computation,
i.e.\ $O(\log^{2}N)$ bit operations. The reduction is therefore \emph{free}
relative to any conceivable cost of producing the congruence.

\begin{principle}[Structural Principle]
All of the difficulty of general-purpose classical factoring is concentrated in
the \emph{production} of a congruence of squares (equivalently, of an even
multiplicative order with a nondegenerate half-power). None of it is in the
exploitation.
\end{principle}

Consequently a classification of factoring methods may be conducted as a
classification of the \emph{resources} used to produce the congruence.

\section{The asymptotic ladder}

Throughout, $x=\log N$ is the bit-size parameter and all growth statements are
as $x\to\infty$ along the reals. Powers $x^{d}$ denote real powers
$\exp(d\log x)$, so exponents range over all of $\mathbb{R}$.

\begin{definition}[Growth classes]
Let $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$.
\begin{itemize}
  \item $f$ is \emph{superpolynomial} if $f(x)/x^{d}\to +\infty$ for every
  $d\in\mathbb{R}$.
  \item $f$ is \emph{subexponential} if $f(x)/e^{\varepsilon x}\to 0$ for every
  $\varepsilon>0$.
  \item $f$ is \emph{polynomially bounded} if $f(x)\le Cx^{d}$ for some
  constants $C,d$ and all large $x$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$L$-function]
For $\alpha,c\in\mathbb{R}$ set
$\Lf[\alpha,c](x):=\exp\!\big(c\,x^{\alpha}(\log x)^{1-\alpha}\big)$. With
$x=\log N$ this is the classical
$L_{N}[\alpha,c]=\exp\big(c(\log N)^{\alpha}(\log\log N)^{1-\alpha}\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotone transfer]\label{lem:transfer}
If $g$ is superpolynomial and $g(x)\le f(x)$ for all large $x$, then $f$ is
superpolynomial.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Fix $d$. For large $x$, $x^{d}>0$ and $g(x)/x^{d}\le f(x)/x^{d}$; a function
eventually dominating one tending to $+\infty$ tends to $+\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Stretched exponentials are superpolynomial]\label{thm:engine}
For all $c>0$ and $\alpha>0$, the map $x\mapsto\exp(c\,x^{\alpha})$ is
superpolynomial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $d$. The limit $e^{cu}/u^{e}\to\infty$, valid for all real $e$ and $c>0$,
applied with $e=d/\alpha$ gives $e^{cu}/u^{d/\alpha}\to\infty$. Compose with
$u=x^{\alpha}\to\infty$ and use $(x^{\alpha})^{d/\alpha}=x^{d}$ for $x>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:explin}
For $b>0$, $x\mapsto e^{bx}$ is superpolynomial.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Lfdom}
For $c\ge 0$, $\alpha\le 1$ and $x\ge e$ we have
$\exp(c\,x^{\alpha})\le \Lf[\alpha,c](x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $x\ge e$ we have $\log x\ge 1$ and $1-\alpha\ge0$, so
$(\log x)^{1-\alpha}\ge 1$. As $cx^{\alpha}\ge0$, multiplying it by a quantity
$\ge 1$ can only increase it; apply monotonicity of $\exp$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Barrier growth]\label{thm:Lsuper}
For $0<\alpha\le 1$ and $c>0$, $\Lf[\alpha,c]$ is superpolynomial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:engine}, Lemma~\ref{lem:Lfdom} and
Lemma~\ref{lem:transfer}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Key growth comparison]\label{lem:keycomp}
For $\alpha<1$, $\dfrac{x^{\alpha}(\log x)^{1-\alpha}}{x}\to 0$ as
$x\to\infty$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $x>1$, using $x^{\alpha}x^{1-\alpha}=x$,
\[
  \frac{x^{\alpha}(\log x)^{1-\alpha}}{x}
  =\frac{(\log x)^{1-\alpha}}{x^{1-\alpha}}
  =\Big(\frac{\log x}{x}\Big)^{1-\alpha}.
\]
Since $(\log x)/x\to0$ and $t\mapsto t^{1-\alpha}$ is continuous at $0$ with
value $0$ (as $1-\alpha>0$), the composite tends to $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Subexponentiality]\label{thm:Lsubexp}
For $c>0$ and $\alpha<1$, $\Lf[\alpha,c]$ is subexponential.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $\varepsilon>0$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:keycomp} there is $X$ with
$x^{\alpha}(\log x)^{1-\alpha}/x<\varepsilon/(2c)$ for $x>X$, i.e.
$c\,x^{\alpha}(\log x)^{1-\alpha}<\tfrac{\varepsilon}{2}x$. Then for such $x$,
\[
  \frac{\Lf[\alpha,c](x)}{e^{\varepsilon x}}
  =\exp\!\big(c\,x^{\alpha}(\log x)^{1-\alpha}-\varepsilon x\big)
  \le \exp\!\big(-\tfrac{\varepsilon}{2}x\big)\xrightarrow[x\to\infty]{}0 .
\]
The left-hand side is positive; the squeeze theorem concludes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Genuine exponentials are not subexponential]\label{thm:expnotsub}
For $b>0$, $x\mapsto e^{bx}$ is not subexponential.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Test $\varepsilon=b/2$: the ratio is $e^{bx}/e^{bx/2}=e^{bx/2}\to\infty$,
contradicting convergence to $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Incompatibility]\label{thm:incompat}
A superpolynomial function is not polynomially bounded.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $f(x)\le Cx^{d}$ eventually then eventually $f(x)/x^{d}\le C$, contradicting
$f(x)/x^{d}\to\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict intermediacy of the $L$-rung]
For $0<\alpha<1$ and $c>0$, $\Lf[\alpha,c]$ is superpolynomial, subexponential
and not polynomially bounded: it occupies a rung strictly above every polynomial
and strictly below every exponential.
\end{theorem}

This is the precise sense in which subexponential factoring algorithms represent
genuine but insufficient progress: they leave the exponential rung entirely, yet
remain infinitely far above the polynomial one.

\section{The four classified resources and their barriers}

\begin{definition}[Classical resources]
$\Res=\{\Rand,\ \Smooth,\ \Iter,\ \Analog\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Barrier cost]\label{def:barrier}
Each resource carries the documented running-time barrier of its representative
methods, expressed in $x=\log N$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Resource & Representative method & Barrier $B_\rho(x)$\\
\midrule
$\Rand$   & Pollard rho, collision search & $\exp(x/4)$, i.e.\ $\Theta(N^{1/4})$\\
$\Smooth$ & CFRAC, quadratic sieve, number field sieve & $\Lf[1/3,1](x)$\\
$\Iter$   & Williams $p+1$, elliptic curve method & $\Lf[1/2,\sqrt2](x/2)$\\
$\Analog$ & analog / chaotic dynamics & $\Lf[1/3,1](x)$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{definition}

The iteration entry is naturally stated in $\log p$; for a balanced semiprime
$\log p\approx x/2$, whence the reparametrization.

\begin{lemma}[Rescaling stability]\label{lem:rescale}
If $f$ is superpolynomial and $b>0$, then $x\mapsto f(x/b)$ is superpolynomial.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Fix $d$. Composing $f(u)/u^{d}\to\infty$ with $u=x/b\to\infty$ gives
$f(x/b)/(x/b)^{d}\to\infty$; dividing by the positive constant $b^{d}$ preserves
divergence, and $(x/b)^{d}b^{d}=x^{d}$ for $x>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Barrier theorem]\label{thm:allsuper}
For every $\rho\in\Res$, the barrier $B_{\rho}$ is superpolynomial. Consequently
no classified barrier is polynomially bounded.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $\Rand$: Corollary~\ref{cor:explin} with $b=1/4$. For $\Smooth$ and
$\Analog$: Theorem~\ref{thm:Lsuper} with $c=1$, $\alpha=1/3$. For $\Iter$:
Theorem~\ref{thm:Lsuper} with $c=\sqrt2>0$, $\alpha=1/2$, followed by
Lemma~\ref{lem:rescale} with $b=2$. The last claim is
Theorem~\ref{thm:incompat}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity]\label{lem:pos}
$B_{\rho}(x)>0$ for all $\rho$ and $x$, each barrier being an exponential.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Non-degeneracy]
The smoothness barrier $\Lf[1/3,1]$ is subexponential; the randomness barrier
$\exp(x/4)$ is not; consequently the two are distinct functions occupying
different rungs of the ladder.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:Lsubexp} with $\alpha=1/3$, $c=1$; Theorem~\ref{thm:expnotsub}
with $b=1/4$. Equality of the functions would transfer subexponentiality, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

The table therefore records genuinely different information: an exponential wall
for collision methods and a strictly lower --- but still superpolynomial ---
wall for sieve and iteration methods.

\section{Why the exponents are $1/k$: the multiplicative trade-off barrier}

\begin{model}[$k$-way trade-off]
A $k$-way trade-off strategy splits its work into $k$ exponential stages of
costs $e^{y_1},\dots,e^{y_k}$, where the budget parameters $y_i>0$ obey a
\emph{multiplicative} constraint $\prod_{i=1}^{k}y_i=x$. Total cost is
$\sum_{i=1}^{k}e^{y_i}$.
\end{model}

The multiplicative constraint is the mathematical content of the sieving
trade-off: lowering the smoothness bound shrinks the factor base and the linear
algebra, but proportionally reduces the density of smooth numbers and increases
the sieving effort. The effects multiply in $\log N$.

\begin{lemma}[AM--GM, balanced weights]\label{lem:amgm}
For $k\ge1$ and nonnegative reals $y_1,\dots,y_k$,
$\big(\prod_i y_i\big)^{1/k}\le \tfrac1k\sum_i y_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative trade-off lower bound]\label{thm:tradeoff}
Let $k\ge1$, $x\in\mathbb{R}$ and $y_1,\dots,y_k>0$ with $\prod_i y_i=x$. Then
\[
  k\exp\!\big(x^{1/k}\big)\ \le\ \sum_{i=1}^{k}e^{y_i}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Two applications of Lemma~\ref{lem:amgm}. First, applied to the budgets and
using $\prod_i y_i=x$, $x^{1/k}\le \tfrac1k\sum_i y_i$. Second, applied to the
costs $e^{y_i}$ and using $\prod_i e^{y_i}=e^{\sum_i y_i}$ together with
$(e^{S})^{1/k}=e^{S/k}$,
$\exp\!\big(\tfrac1k\sum_i y_i\big)\le \tfrac1k\sum_i e^{y_i}$. Chaining the
first through the monotone $\exp$ into the second gives
$\exp(x^{1/k})\le\tfrac1k\sum_i e^{y_i}$; multiply by $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
For $k\ge1$ and $x>0$, the balanced choice $y_1=\dots=y_k=x^{1/k}$ satisfies
$\prod_i y_i=x$ and attains the bound: $\sum_i e^{y_i}=k\exp(x^{1/k})$.
\end{theorem}

Thus the optimal $k$-way trade-off cost is \emph{exactly} $k\exp(x^{1/k})$. The
exponent $1/k$ is not a design parameter but the AM--GM balance point of the
constraint. Two consequences follow: ``improving the exponent'' is literally the
same operation as ``adding a stage'' --- historically, exactly what the number
field sieve did to the quadratic sieve --- and polynomial time is the
$k\to\infty$ limit of the family.

\begin{theorem}[Fixed arity cannot reach polynomial time]
For each fixed $k\ge1$, $x\mapsto k\exp(x^{1/k})$ is superpolynomial, hence not
polynomially bounded. In particular both $2\exp(x^{1/2})$ and $3\exp(x^{1/3})$
--- the quadratic-sieve/ECM and number-field-sieve regimes --- are
superpolynomial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\exp(1\cdot x^{1/k})$ is superpolynomial by Theorem~\ref{thm:engine} with
$\alpha=1/k>0$; multiplying by $k\ge1$ only increases it, so
Lemma~\ref{lem:transfer} applies. Then Theorem~\ref{thm:incompat}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary of the trade-off barrier]\label{thm:boundary}
For every $x>e$ there exists $k\ge1$ with
$k\exp\!\big(x^{1/k}\big)\le e^{e}(\log x+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $k=\lceil \log x\rceil\ge1$, so $\log x\le k\le \log x+1$. Then
$x^{1/k}=\exp(\log x/k)\le \exp(1)=e$, whence
$k\exp(x^{1/k})\le k\,e^{e}\le(\log x+1)e^{e}$.
\end{proof}

The right-hand side is $O(\log x)$, hence polynomially bounded. We state this
rather than hide it: the trade-off barrier is a theorem about \emph{bounded}
arity. A strategy balancing unboundedly many stages simultaneously escapes it,
and no known classical method supplies the structure to do so.

\section{The randomness barrier, made worst-case rigorous}

Pollard's rho method and its relatives compute $\gcd(x_i-x_j,N)$ for iterates of
some map. The quoted $\Theta(N^{1/4})$ running time is a \emph{birthday
heuristic}: it assumes iterates behave like uniform random residues modulo the
unknown prime $p\approx\sqrt N$. Two unconditional theorems sit underneath.

\begin{theorem}[Collisions are necessary]\label{thm:collnec}
Let $p,q$ be primes and $a,b\in\mathbb{Z}$ with $p\nmid(a-b)$ and $q\nmid(a-b)$.
Then $\gcd(a-b,\,pq)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $d=\gcd(a-b,pq)$; then $d\mid pq$ and $d\mid(a-b)$, and $d\ne0$ since
$pq\ne0$. If $d>1$, choose a prime $r\mid d$; then $r\mid pq$ forces $r\in\{p,q\}$,
and $r\mid(a-b)$ contradicts a hypothesis. Hence $d=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic trajectory blindness]\label{thm:blind}
Let $p,q$ be primes and $K\le\min(p,q)$. Then for all distinct
$i,j\in\{0,1,\dots,K-1\}$ we have $\gcd(i-j,\,pq)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$i\ne j$ gives $i-j\ne0$, and $0\le i,j<K$ gives $|i-j|<K\le\min(p,q)$. If
$p\mid(i-j)$ then $p\le|i-j|<p$, absurd; likewise for $q$. Apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:collnec}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Worst-case waste]
For a semiprime $pq$ with both prime factors at least $B$, every pair of
distinct points of the arithmetic trajectory of length $B$ fails to produce a
nontrivial divisor; a collision-based search can be made to waste $B$ steps.
\end{corollary}

For a balanced semiprime, $\min(p,q)\approx\sqrt N$. Hence the provable
\emph{worst-case} lower bound for collision-based factoring is $\sqrt N$, not
$N^{1/4}$: the fourth-root running time is an average-case phenomenon about
pseudorandom trajectories, not a theorem about all trajectories. We nonetheless
retain $\exp(x/4)$ in Definition~\ref{def:barrier}, since the
conditional-impossibility argument requires only a superpolynomial barrier and
the smaller value is the conservative choice.

\section{The information-theoretic Fourier sample bound}

Period-finding-based factoring extracts the order $r$ of $a$ modulo $N$ from the
discrete Fourier transform of a period-$r$ signal. A recurring hope for a
``classical Shor'' is that \emph{few} Fourier samples might suffice. This is
impossible, in a representation-independent form depending only on
$\mathbb{C}$-linearity of Fourier sampling.

\begin{theorem}[Linear measurement dimension bound]\label{thm:dim}
Let $V,W$ be $\mathbb{C}$-vector spaces with $W$ finite-dimensional and let
$M:V\to W$ be $\mathbb{C}$-linear with $\dim W<\dim V$. Then there exist $v\ne w$
in $V$ with $Mv=Mw$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $M$ separated all pairs it would be injective, whence $\dim V\le\dim W$,
contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

Let $r\ge1$. The signal space $\mathbb{C}^{\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}}$ has dimension
$r$. For frequencies $\iota:\{1,\dots,K\}\to\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$ define the
sampling map
\[
  S_{\iota}:\mathbb{C}^{\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}}\to\mathbb{C}^{K},\qquad
  (S_{\iota}v)_{j}=\widehat{v}(\iota(j)),
\]
the composition of the (linear, invertible) transform with a coordinate
restriction; its codomain has dimension $K$.

\begin{theorem}[Fewer than $r$ samples are blind]\label{thm:blindsamples}
If $K<r$, then for \emph{any} choice of $K$ sample frequencies there exist
distinct signals $v\ne w$ with $\widehat{v}(\iota(j))=\widehat{w}(\iota(j))$ for
all $j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dim} to $S_{\iota}$, using $r>K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sample lower bound]\label{thm:Kger}
If a family of $K$ sample frequencies is \emph{determining} --- any two signals
agreeing at all $K$ sampled frequencies of their transforms are equal --- then
$K\ge r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:blindsamples}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
Sampling at all $r$ frequencies is determining: the discrete Fourier transform
on $\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$ is a linear isomorphism, hence injective. The bound
$K\ge r$ is attained.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:Kger} is unconditional and independent of the computational
model: it constrains any procedure whose access to the signal is through linear
Fourier measurements at chosen frequencies, however those frequencies are
computed and however the outcomes are post-processed. Since $r$ is typically
comparable to $N$, a classical sampling strategy must pay $\Omega(N)$
measurements. Quantum superposition evades this: a single state carries all $r$
amplitudes and the quantum Fourier transform acts on all of them at once. The
bound therefore identifies exactly which capability separates the quantum route
from every sampling-based classical imitation.

\section{The conditional-impossibility schema}

\begin{definition}[Classical algorithm, abstracted]
A \emph{classical algorithm} is a cost profile $C:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$, where
$C(x)$ is the running time on inputs of bit-size $x=\log N$, subject to the
normalization $C(x)\ge1$ for all large $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
An algorithm $A$ with cost $C$ \emph{runs in polynomial time} if $C$ is
polynomially bounded; is \emph{limited by} $\rho\in\Res$ if $B_{\rho}(x)\le C(x)$
for all large $x$; and \emph{uses a classified resource} if it is limited by
some $\rho\in\Res$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Step 1: strict beating]\label{thm:step1}
If $A$ runs in polynomial time then for every $\rho\in\Res$, $C(x)<B_{\rho}(x)$
for all large $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $C(x)\le Kx^{d}$ eventually. By Theorem~\ref{thm:allsuper},
$B_{\rho}(x)/x^{d}\to\infty$, so eventually $(K+1)x^{d}<B_{\rho}(x)$. For $x>1$
we have $x^{d}>0$, so $C(x)\le Kx^{d}<(K+1)x^{d}<B_{\rho}(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Step 2: no barrier applies]\label{thm:step2}
If $A$ runs in polynomial time then $A$ is limited by no $\rho\in\Res$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both ``limited by $\rho$'' and Theorem~\ref{thm:step1} hold eventually, hence
simultaneously at some $x$, giving $B_{\rho}(x)\le C(x)<B_{\rho}(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional impossibility]\label{thm:condimp}
If a classical algorithm factors semiprimes with cost polynomially bounded in
$\log N$, then it does not use a classified resource: the resource it exploits
lies outside $\{\Rand,\Smooth,\Iter,\Analog\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
An algorithm confined to the classified resources cannot run in polynomial time.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded gap]\label{thm:gap}
If $A$ runs in polynomial time then for every $\rho\in\Res$,
$\lim_{x\to\infty} B_{\rho}(x)/C(x)=+\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $C(x)\le Kx^{d}$ eventually with $K>0$ (positivity follows from
$C(x)\ge1$ at a point where the bound holds). Using Lemma~\ref{lem:pos} and
Theorem~\ref{thm:allsuper},
\[
  \frac{B_{\rho}(x)}{C(x)}\ \ge\ \frac{1}{K}\cdot\frac{B_{\rho}(x)}{x^{d}}
  \xrightarrow[x\to\infty]{}\infty. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\subsection{Level 3: scope, stated as a hypothesis}

\begin{definition}[Classified Resource Hypothesis, CRH]
Every classical factoring algorithm is limited by one of the four classified
barriers.
\end{definition}

We emphasise that CRH is \emph{not} proved here and should not be regarded as
established. It is a statement about the space of all algorithms, including
those not yet invented. What we prove are its consequences and its
falsifiability.

\begin{theorem}[CRH implies hardness]
If CRH holds, then no classical factoring algorithm runs in polynomial time.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Falsifiability]
If some classical factoring algorithm runs in polynomial time, then CRH is
false.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Non-vacuity]
\leavevmode
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The abstract algorithm whose cost profile \emph{is} $B_{\Smooth}$ uses a
  classified resource (by equality) and is not polynomial-time. Its cost meets
  the normalization since $B_{\rho}(x)/x^{0}=B_{\rho}(x)\to\infty$.
  \item The cost profile $C(x)=x^{2}$ is polynomially bounded and uses no
  classified resource.
\end{enumerate}
Thus both predicates are satisfiable and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:condimp},
mutually exclusive.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Capstone]
Let $A$ be a classical factoring algorithm running in polynomial time in
$\log N$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
  \item for every classified resource $\rho$, $B_{\rho}(x)/C(x)\to\infty$;
  \item $A$ is limited by none of $\{\Rand,\Smooth,\Iter,\Analog\}$;
  \item $A$ does not use a classified resource;
  \item the Classified Resource Hypothesis is false --- the resource $A$
  exploits lies outside the classified catalogue.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorems~\ref{thm:gap}, \ref{thm:step2}, \ref{thm:condimp}, and falsifiability
respectively.
\end{proof}

This is a rigorous conditional: it derives strong structural consequences from
the hypothetical existence of a fast classical algorithm, \emph{without}
asserting that no such algorithm exists.

\section{The quantum boundary}

The framework classifies classical resources only. Two unconditional facts point
in opposite directions: the classical post-processing reduction from order
finding to factorization (Theorem~\ref{thm:order}) is free; and the only
information-theoretic obstruction we can establish for Fourier-based period
determination is the sample-count bound $K\ge r$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:Kger}), which superposition supplies in one shot.

\begin{theorem}[Boundary statement]
Let $N>1$, $a\in\mathbb{Z}$, $s\in\mathbb{N}$ with $N\mid a^{2s}-1$ and
$N\nmid a^{s}\mp1$; let $r\ge1$ and let $\iota$ be a determining family of $K$
Fourier sample frequencies on $\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$. Then simultaneously
$\gcd(a^{s}-1,N)$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N$, and $K\ge r$.
\end{theorem}

Once the period is known, factoring is trivial; and the only classical route to
the period through Fourier measurement demands a number of measurements equal to
the period itself.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the framework establishes}

Every resource known to classical factoring runs into a provably
superpolynomial wall; the exponents in those walls are forced balance points
rather than tunable parameters; the natural classical imitation of the quantum
route is blocked by a dimension count. Therefore a polynomial-time classical
factoring algorithm would necessarily exploit a resource outside the entire
known catalogue. The caveat: this is a classification of the known, not a proof
that the unknown is empty.

\subsection{Why the honest framing has value}

Resisting the temptation to present a classification of known methods as a lower
bound costs nothing and gains a great deal. What remains is a falsifiable
hypothesis that a single algorithm would refute; a specification for a
breakthrough, telling a would-be breaker of RSA precisely what to bring; and a
separation of mathematics from folklore, of which the correction of the
collision barrier from $N^{1/4}$ to $\sqrt N$ in the worst case is a concrete
instance.

\subsection{Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Exhaustiveness is unproved.} CRH is a hypothesis; the framework
  is silent on resources outside the catalogue --- that silence is its subject.
  \item \textbf{The abstraction is coarse.} Algorithms are modelled as cost
  profiles, so fine-grained statements about memory or parallelism are
  inexpressible.
  \item \textbf{The trade-off model is a model.} Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff}
  characterises the optimum of a specific cost model; that it captures sieving
  is an interpretive claim supported by the match with the observed exponents.
  \item \textbf{The trade-off barrier has an explicit boundary}
  (Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary}).
  \item \textbf{The Fourier bound constrains sampling, not computation.}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to the broader landscape}

The framework is complementary to, and logically independent of, the standard
complexity-theoretic picture. Factoring lies in
$\mathsf{NP}\cap\mathsf{coNP}$, so it is not $\mathsf{NP}$-hard unless the
polynomial hierarchy collapses, and conventional hardness assumptions give no
leverage. What is available is exactly what this framework makes precise: an
accounting of resources and the walls they hit.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{The trade-off arity conjecture}

Define the \emph{arity} of a cost profile $f$ as the least $k$ with
$f(x)\le k\exp(c\,x^{1/k})$ for some $c>0$ and all large $x$.

\begin{conjecture}[Arity separation]
The arity classes are strictly nested: for every $k$ there is a cost profile of
arity $k+1$ that is not $O$ of any arity-$k$ profile; and a profile is
polynomially bounded if and only if it has no finite arity.
\end{conjecture}

The motivation is Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff}: the exponent $1/k$ is the balance
point of a multiplicative constraint, so ``improving the exponent'' and ``adding
a stage'' are the same operation, and polynomial time is the $k\to\infty$ limit
(Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary}). Both endpoints are established; only strictness of
the intermediate steps is open, and it reduces to a limit computation of the
kind carried out in Section~3. \emph{Falsifier:} exhibit $c,c',k$ with
$(k+1)\exp(c\,x^{1/(k+1)})=O\big(k\exp(c'x^{1/k})\big)$.

\subsection{Barrier closure under algorithmic composition}

\begin{conjecture}[Barrier closure]
The class of algorithms limited by a classified barrier is closed under
composition, sequential repetition and polynomial-time preprocessing: if $A$ is
limited by $\rho$ and $B$ runs in polynomial time, then $x\mapsto
C_{A}(x)+C_{B}(p(x))$ is still limited by $\rho$ for every polynomial
reparametrization $p$ bounded below by a positive power of $x$.
\end{conjecture}

The motivation is Lemma~\ref{lem:rescale}, already what legitimises the ECM
entry. Closure is exactly what makes CRH a statement about a robust class rather
than about a list. \emph{Falsifier:} a reparametrization $p$ and superpolynomial
$f$ with $f\circ p$ polynomially bounded --- e.g.\ any sub-logarithmic $p$; the
conjecture must therefore restrict $p$, and the question is whether the
restriction suffices.

\subsection{Further directions}

Proving the $\Theta(N^{1/4})$ collision figure as a theorem requires a rigorous
equidistribution statement for the iterates actually used, and would close the
framework's largest gap between folklore and theorem. Beyond sampling, a
classical algorithm determining the period by nonlinear or algebraic access is
not excluded by Theorem~\ref{thm:Kger}; characterising such access and seeking
an analogous obstruction is the natural next target. Finally, the four resources
are currently a list; an algebra of resources --- operations, invariants, and
closure --- would turn the classification into a structure theory of classical
factoring effort.

\section{Conclusion}

Unconditionally: the congruence-of-squares reduction is free, so all difficulty
lies in producing the congruence; the subexponential complexity functions occupy
a strictly intermediate rung between polynomial and exponential growth; every
classified barrier is superpolynomial and the classification is non-degenerate;
the sieve exponents $1/k$ are AM--GM balance points, not design choices, and no
bounded-arity trade-off reaches polynomial time; collision methods are provably
blind for $\min(p,q)$ steps in the worst case; and any determining family of
Fourier sample frequencies for a period-$r$ signal has at least $r$ members.

Conditionally: any polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm beats each
classified barrier by an unbounded factor, is limited by none of them, and
therefore exploits a resource outside the classified catalogue --- falsifying
the Classified Resource Hypothesis.

And honestly: the Classified Resource Hypothesis is a hypothesis. The framework
maps the walls we know. It does not claim there are no doors.

\end{document}

